// C1.7 / ADR 0016, register D45: a bare `null` ARGUMENT to a generic call takes its type
// from the parameter it fills, substituted with what the arguments to its left bound.
//
// `takes<T>(d: T, x: ?T)` called as `takes(true, null)`: `d` binds T = bool, so the
// `null` is `?bool` and renders `{ i1, i1 } { i1 0, i1 0 }`. `scg` dumped arguments with
// no expected type and unified only afterwards, so the `null` — whose type IS its
// expectation — fell back to a default and rendered `i64 { i1 0, i64 0 }`.
//
// ⚠ T = bool ON PURPOSE. At T = i64 the wrong answer and the right answer coincide, and
// the register entry was written from that degenerate case: it describes only a wrong
// "type prefix", when at any other T the whole thing is wrong — and with the `null`
// FIRST instead, the wrong default also binds T and the entire monomorphisation key
// changes (`@takes__i64` for `@takes__bool`, wrong signature, wrong body). Pinning this
// at i64 would have pinned nothing.
//
// ⚠ THE `null`-FIRST ORDER IS STILL BROKEN and is deliberately NOT pinned here — it
// cannot be, because a `tests/pass` fixture must be byte-identical. Fixing it needs the
// argument's already-emitted MIR value and LLVM operand slot to be PATCHED after the
// walk (a bare `null` costs no LLVM register but DOES take a positional MIR slot, so it
// cannot simply be deferred). See the register.
//
// This half is the LOUD one: pre-fix `llvm-as` rejects it with "constant expression type
// mismatch". Its twin `c17_generic_arg_pushdown.sentinel` is the SILENT half. Keep both.
//
// 40 + 2 = 42.
fn takes<T>(d: T, x: ?T) -> i64 {
    if is_some(x) { 0 } else { 40 }
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    takes(true, null) + 2
}
